' AsyncTask — the one choreography every off-the-UI-thread load used to
' hand-roll: create a <X>Task node, append it, set its fields, observe result,
' run it, then reap it (drop the observer, STOP the worker, remove the node)
' once the observer fires or the load is cancelled.
'
' Usage, matching the pre-refactor shape exactly:
'
'   task = AsyncTask_Launch(m.top, "MetaLoaderTask", "onMetaLoaded", {
'       addonAddress: address
'       metaType: meta.type
'       metaId: meta.id
'   }, "detailsMetaLoader")
'   m.loadTask = task
'
' Reaping a finished task in the observer handler:
'
'   if m.loadTask = invalid then return
'   task = m.loadTask
'   m.loadTask = invalid
'   AsyncTask_Reap(task, m.top, false)
'
' Cancelling a possibly-still-running task:
'
'   AsyncTask_Reap(task, m.top, true)   ' STOP must come first — removing a
'                                       ' running Task node does not kill its
'                                       ' worker thread.
'
' Components must include this file to see it:
'   <script type="text/brightscript" uri="pkg:/source/core/AsyncTask.bs" />

' Launch a task node and run it. Returns the node so the caller can slot it for
' stale-result guards. parent is the screen node the task child is appended to;
' callback is the observer for the task's "result" field; fields is the
' interface values to set before the run; id is an optional node identity.
'
' doneCallback is optional and only for tasks that carry a "finished" sentinel
' field (see AddonSyncTask). Pass it when "result = invalid" is AMBIGUOUS for the
' caller — an alwaysNotify field with no value can notify before the worker has
' written anything, and a handler that treats that as "the task produced
' nothing" destroys the real answer: AsyncTask_Reap unobserves the field and
' removes the node, and removing a running Task does NOT kill its worker thread,
' so the genuine result lands on a node nobody is watching. With a sentinel the
' caller can decline to act on an empty result and let the done-callback be the
' authority on whether the worker really finished without one. Both observers are
' attached before control = "RUN", which is the only point at which the worker
' can possibly start, so neither can miss its first notification.
'
' `fields` is assigned key by key, and THAT is a typed boundary: roSGNode's
' index-set goes through AddReplace, which enforces the field's declared type and
' raises at runtime on anything else. So the declared type in the task's XML is
' not documentation — an array assigned to a `type="assocarray"` field is a hard
' "Type mismatch" on the device, and a float into `type="integer"` is the same
' hazard one step removed.
'
' Two things follow, and both cost a deploy to learn the hard way:
'
'   Nesting is not a loophole. A list inside the `stream` assocarray would be
'   fine, and a list assigned to a field declared assocarray is not — the check
'   is on the top-level value, not on what it contains. When a task needs more
'   than one scalar, add fields.
'
'   Nothing in the test suite sees this. The brs interpreter does not enforce
'   field types, so a mismatched assignment is green here and broken on the
'   device. A declared type can only be checked by reading the XML against the
'   call site, which no test currently does.
function AsyncTask_Launch(parent as object, taskName as string, callback as string, fields as object, id as dynamic, doneCallback = "" as string) as object
    task = CreateObject("roSGNode", taskName)
    if id <> invalid and id <> "" then task.id = id
    parent.AppendChild(task)
    if fields <> invalid
        for each key in fields
            task[key] = fields[key]
        end for
    end if
    task.ObserveField("result", callback)
    if doneCallback <> "" then task.ObserveField("finished", doneCallback)
    task.control = "RUN"
    return task
end function

' Tear a task down: drop the observers so a late result can never land, then
' (when stop) send STOP so a still-running worker's read-loop exits before the
' node is freed, then remove it from the tree. "finished" is unobserved too; for
' the thirteen tasks with no sentinel that is a no-op.
sub AsyncTask_Reap(task as object, parent as object, doStop as boolean)
    if task = invalid then return
    task.UnobserveField("result")
    task.UnobserveField("finished")
    if doStop then task.control = "STOP"
    if task.getParent() <> invalid then parent.RemoveChild(task)
end sub